// std/rand.momo - a 16-bit xorshift generator.
//
// Good enough for a game, not for anything else. Period 65535, and - unlike the
// LCG this replaced - **every bit is as good as every other**, so callers do not
// have to know which ones to take.
//
// That is the whole reason for the change. A power-of-two-modulus LCG has
// short-period low bits: bit k repeats every 2^(k+1) calls. The consequences
// were neither obvious nor small:
//
//   randomBelow(2)            period 4     - a coin flip that cycles H H T T
//   randomBelow(16)           period 32
//   u8(nextRandom())          period 256
//   u8(nextRandom() >> 1)     period 512   - a visible band every 1.6 scanlines
//
// Non-power-of-two arguments were fine, which is why nothing noticed. All of the
// above are 65535 now. Statistically the two generators are a wash at this state
// width; it is the absence of weak bits that is worth having.
//
// **It is about twice the cost** - roughly 400 cycles against 207 on an 8086 -
// and that is worth paying for a routine a game calls a few times a frame, but
// worth knowing before calling it in a loop. Two thirds of the difference is
// codegen rather than algorithm: `x ^ (x << 7)` reloads `x` and takes the
// push/pop path because the right side is not a leaf, and `shl ax, cl` costs
// 8 + 4n where seven separate `shl ax, 1` would cost 14. §21's unroll would
// bring it to about 1.5x. A 64000-pixel fill notices; nothing else will.
//
// A wider state is the only way to do materially better: 16 bits caps
// consecutive-pair coverage at about half of the 65536 possible. Fine for dice,
// loot and combat. Procedural noise wants something else entirely - a positional
// hash, a pure function of coordinates rather than a stream - which is a
// different primitive, not a better version of this one.

// `local`, so the state is reachable only from this file. It was public until
// there was a way to say otherwise, which left the guard below with a hole in
// it: a program could write `randomSeed = 0` directly and walk past the one
// check that keeps the generator alive.
local u16 randomSeed = 42

// Zero is a fixed point: xorshift would return it forever. Mapped rather than
// rejected, so seeding can never silently kill the generator.
sub seedRandom(u16 s) => randomSeed = s == 0 ? 1 : s

// Shifts of 7, 9, 8 give the full 65535-state cycle. Arithmetic is 16-bit
// throughout, so the left shifts truncate exactly as the algorithm wants.
u16 nextRandom() {
  randomSeed = randomSeed ^ (randomSeed << 7)
  randomSeed = randomSeed ^ (randomSeed >> 9)
  randomSeed = randomSeed ^ (randomSeed << 8)
  return randomSeed
}

// No bit-picking needed any more - the low bits carry the full period, so the
// modulo takes the value as it comes. `n` must not be zero: an 8086 divide by
// zero raises INT 0.
u16 randomBelow(u16 n) => nextRandom() % n

// Inclusive on both ends.
u16 randomBetween(u16 low, u16 high) => low + randomBelow(high - low + 1)
